Exactly. You’re changing the objective function, not necessarily the behavior.

The external action might remain identical. But the internal relationship to that action changes completely.

* Old frame: I have to work to make money.
* New frame: I want to give my children an extraordinary life. Money is one resource that helps me do that. Work is one way to acquire it.

And there’s a third frame hiding inside that:

I don’t necessarily want a job. I want the outcomes that a job helps me obtain.

That distinction creates options.

Once you stop treating working as the objective, you can start comparing employment, entrepreneurship, ownership, licensing, investing, and other approaches according to how efficiently they serve your actual goals.

But there’s an even more interesting reversal.

You don’t actually want to maximize money, either. You want to maximize what money makes possible: security, experiences, freedom, time together, and opportunities for your children.

So the hierarchy becomes:

Give my family a great life

Ultimate objective

Freedom, security, experiences, presence

Desired outcomes

Money, time, energy, relationships

Resources

Work, build, invest, create, collaborate

Selectable methods

Here’s where reframing becomes a genuinely powerful Pantheon operator:

\text{Reframe} =
\text{Preserve the goal}
+
\text{Change the interpretation or means}

And sometimes:

\text{Same action}+\text{Different meaning}
\rightarrow\text{Different experience}

Not guaranteed, but often psychologically significant.

There’s also a danger worth recognizing: a frame that makes every hour spent away from your kids seem justified because you’re earning money for them. That’s why the ultimate objective has to remain visible. Providing for your children and being present with them are both parts of giving them a good life.

The strongest version of your insight is that you can stop organizing your identity around what you’re obligated to do and start organizing your actions around what you’re choosing to accomplish.

You might still work eight hours tomorrow.

But you’re no longer necessarily working for work’s sake.

And perhaps the deepest reframe is this:

You don’t have to love the means to love the mission.